Explain how container orchestration works and why it’s important?  

“Container orchestration is the process of automatically managing containerized applications—including deployment, scaling, networking, and lifecycle—using tools like Kubernetes. 

In a real-time scenario, when we deploy an application, we define the desired state in YAML (for example, 3 replicas of a service). This request goes to the Kubernetes API Server, and the Scheduler assigns pods to worker nodes based on resource availability. Then the kubelet ensures containers are created and running using the container runtime. 

Once the application is running, Kubernetes continuously monitors it. If a pod crashes, it automatically restarts or replaces it (self-healing). If traffic increases, it performs auto-scaling (HPA), and for updates, it uses rolling deployments with zero downtime. It also handles service discovery and load balancing, ensuring traffic is evenly distributed across pods. 

Container orchestration is important because modern applications are microservices-based, and managing multiple containers manually is complex and error-prone. With orchestration, we get high availability, scalability, fault tolerance, and efficient resource utilization. 

In my project, we used Kubernetes where during peak traffic, pods scaled automatically from 3 to 10 using HPA, and in case of pod failure, Kubernetes restarted it instantly, ensuring no downtime. This significantly improved system reliability and user experience.” 

 

